{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\" lines=\"97-117\">\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b74ca5d1\" lines=\"157-172\">\n<description>\nSwap each 5x5 sprite with its corner marker color and stamp same-marker composite masks into the matching corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- A 30x30 grid has one dominant background color.\n- Non-background corner pixels are marker labels.\n- Every other foreground color is the body of one 5x5 sprite containing exactly one pixel of a corner marker color.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, recolor body pixels to the sprite marker color and recolor the marker pixel back to its body color.\n- If a marker lies on the side facing its matching corner and another same-marker sprite has a body pixel at that same local cell, use that visible body color for the marker pixel.\n- For each corner marker, overlay the masks of all sprites with that marker color into the corner 5x5 block and paint the visible composite with the marker color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe corner pixels are labels that group 5x5 sprites by their embedded marker color; each labeled corner shows the grouped sprite silhouette.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.\n- Write black everywhere else in the block.\n\nKey insight\nEach pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"689c358e\" lines=\"72-88\">\n<description>\nMark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.\n- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.\n- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.\n- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.\n- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"33067df9\" lines=\"32-48\">\n<description>\nExpand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.\n- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.\n\nRule\n- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.\n- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.\n- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.\n\nKey insight\n- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f8de559\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nErase the first border pixel hit by extending the red marker away from its pink trail.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds a unique red marker and a four-pixel\n  pink trail.\n- The pink pixels form one straight 8-connected ray starting next to the\n  red marker.\n- In the opposite direction from that trail, the red marker faces a\n  non-background border or obstacle pixel beyond background cells.\n\nRule\n- Keep the whole grid fixed except for the first non-background pixel on\n  the ray from the red marker away from the pink trail.\n- Recolor that one hit pixel to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The pink trail marks where the red marker came from, so the edit is found\n  by ray-casting from the red marker in the opposite direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n<description>\nComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n\nRule\n- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n\nKey insight\n- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"878187ab\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nReplace the scattered foreground markers with a bottom-left count rectangle whose two corner diagonals encode the marker counts.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with exactly two foreground colors used as scattered marker pixels.\n- The two foreground colors have distinct pixel counts.\n\nRule\n- Ignore marker positions and use only the two foreground counts.\n- Make a 16-by-16 background canvas, fill a bottom-left red rectangle whose height is the smaller count and width is the larger count.\n- Paint yellow pixels along the two rising 45-degree diagonals from the rectangle's bottom-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker colors and positions vary between examples; their counts alone determine the output rectangle dimensions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4e7e0eb9\" lines=\"31-49\">\n<description>\nTransform each 9x9 panel by mirroring across its center guide line or filling all four block slots from its lone exemplar.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is made of 9x9 panels separated by one-cell-wide grid lines. Each\npanel has four monochrome 3x3 slots at the quadrant positions, with either a\nfull non-background center row, a full non-background center column, or no\nguide line.\n\nRule\nA panel with a full center column is reflected left-to-right, and a panel\nwith a full center row is reflected top-to-bottom. A panel with no guide line\nhas exactly one slot color that appears once and one slot color that appears\nthree times; recolor all four slots to the once-occurring slot color.\n\nKey insight\nThe center guide line encodes the mirror axis, while panels without a guide\nline are completed by copying the structurally unique slot color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
